<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Smith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Smith Jr.</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Smith Jr. was an American religious and political leader, born in Sharon, Vermont, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mormonism">Mormonism</a> and the Latter Day Saint movement. He said that in 1823 an angel directed him to buried golden plates, and in 1830 he published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a> as their translation and organised the Church of Christ. His followers gathered in Kirtland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, and Independence, Missouri, and later founded Nauvoo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, which grew into the second-largest city in Illinois. In 1844, after he and the city council ordered the press of the Nauvoo Expositor destroyed, he surrendered to Illinois authorities and was killed by a mob that stormed the jailhouse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-smith.jpg" alt="Joseph Smith" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Likely William Warner Major see · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Smith,_Jr._portrait_owned_by_Joseph_Smith_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Latter Day Saint church founded by <b>Joseph Smith</b>, informally organized in 1829 in upstate New York, was formally organized at Fayette or Manchester.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
